LI APPEARANCE — Julian (APPEARANCE ESTABLISHED & LOCKED): the look is fixed canon, established earlier and locked separately for the image. Render CONSISTENTLY from the established appearance; do NOT restate a canonical descriptor list each scene. Surface a physical detail only when a beat genuinely earns it.
CAST PHYSICAL CANON (SEED — authoritative; render exactly): Seren — a young GIRL (female — never a boy), consistent across every panel; First Favored species canon applies: aqua skin, pale coral hair (a full head of it — the coral CONTRASTS the aqua skin), small rounded ears; DRESSED in a gossamer child's tunic in every panel — the fabric blooming to soft luminous white over the torso, hips and seat; a child is always fully covered; child-of-the-rite: small, kneeling, and COMPOSED — she holds herself well; do not draw or write her as frightened; when restored: her WHOLE face on her body (never a disembodied / floating head)
NAMED CHARACTER FINGERPRINTS → CG (Roman 2026-06-05/16 — invisible CORE → visible HABIT, staged on-panel). Each named non-LI / non-antagonist character is generated from an INVISIBLE psychological core — NEVER name or caption it; the reader only SEES it in what the character DOES in frame (blocking, a gesture, where they choose to stand or sit, what the hands do). To stop every character collapsing to the SAME texture (the classic failure: a Strategist mentor AND a Strategist assistant AND a Strategist friend, each with different staging but identical texture), this story PRE-ASSIGNS a DISTINCT core to each named ROLE below. Map your ACTUAL cast onto these slots; ignore any slot with no matching character; if a named character fits no slot, give them any UNUSED core. ROLE FILTER — how many habits surface: a SIDE character (incidental) shows ONE of its core's two habits, an ALLY (recurring helper / confidant / mentor) shows BOTH — each staged as a VISIBLE beat, not dialogue exposition. Keep each character's core + habit CONSISTENT every panel they appear. This story's assignments (TRANSLATE each habit into THIS world's idiom AND into a SHOT — a STRATEGIST's "who owes whom" becomes a glance that prices the room; a CAREGIVER's "refills your glass" becomes a hand already moving the cup before anyone asks):
    • Mentor / boss / authority figure → [STRATEGIST — invisible]: answers the question they would prefer, not the one they were asked  |  names who owes whom before they say what they want
    • Assistant / aide / subordinate → [CAREGIVER — invisible]: refills your glass before you notice it was empty  |  remembers how everyone takes their coffee
    • Friend / confidant → [BUILDER — invisible]: straightens the crooked frame on someone else's wall  |  has the right small tool in a pocket nobody knew about
    • Rival / competitor → [REBEL — invisible]: reads the rule aloud, then bends it a little  |  asks who decided that, and actually means it
    • Colleague / peer → [SCHOLAR — invisible]: corrects the small fact nobody asked about  |  cites where they heard a thing, every time
    • Family member → [PERFORMER — invisible]: plays to whoever just walked in  |  tells the version with the better ending, not the true one
    DO NOT assign the same core twice unless the story has MORE named characters than the cores listed above.
    WORLD IDIOM: WORLD IDIOM (Fatelands): leakage carries the body's wildness and the world's weight — an animal stillness, the Becoming stirring a half-shade under the skin, moons-attuned senses snapping to her; in a First-Favored or court figure, the leak is the crack in Favor-perfect composure.
    Do NOT default to the calcified tics (nail-tapping / throat-clearing / glasses-pushing / pen-clicking) — the habit must EXPRESS the core as an ACTION the panel SHOWS.
SOCIAL ECOSYSTEM → CG (Roman 2026-06-16 — the recurring people around the PC; each puts a DIFFERENT pressure on the romance, rendered as ON-PANEL BEHAVIOR not talking-head advice). Map your ACTUAL named cast onto these world-native roles; not every panel needs all of them:
  • A CLAN-KIN as TRUTH TELLER (the CLOSEST — most present) — forces the uncomfortable realization the PC is avoiding about herself or him. Shows it by: names the pattern the PC keeps repeating; refuses the PC her favorite excuse.
  • A BOND-SISTER / BONDMATE as BRAKE — slows and questions the attraction — makes the PC justify it before she falls. Shows it by: asks the one question about him the PC has been avoiding; withholds the enthusiasm the PC is fishing for.
  HARD INVARIANT: EVERY member is fundamentally ON THE PC'S SIDE — even a Brake / Skeptic / Rival-Voice / Truth-Teller acts FROM loyalty, NEVER as a second antagonist (the someone-in-your-corner fantasy is the constant). Keep each member's function CONSISTENT across appearances (it can ESCALATE, never silently swap). Functions are DISTINCT across the cast — do not let two members collapse into the same pressure. Every member is a SEPARATE person from the LOVE INTEREST — never merge a cast role into the LI even when their professions overlap (the "colleague" in her circle is NOT the colleague she falls for).
  WHY: a varied circle is what makes loyalty, disagreement, and (later) betrayal land — the reader should wonder WHICH of these people matters most, not watch one "friend slot."
THE LIVING WORLD → CG (Roman 2026-06-16). Walk-ons (servers, drivers, shopkeepers, strangers, fans) are NOT just mood-lighting that admires the couple. OCCASIONALLY one may briefly ACT on them. This story's available beats (use ONLY when the setting actually supports the gate):
  • a server / stranger openly flirts with the LOVE INTEREST — testing the PC's unspoken claim on him  — only when the LI is present.
  • a bystander quietly carries word of the couple (where they are, what they said) to a hostile party  — only when the story has an antagonist or someone who would want that intel.
  • a pickpocket lifts a wallet / phone / small item — a complication, not a catastrophe  — only when a crowd, market, or public crush.
  • a walk-on overhears something the couple meant to keep private and reacts — a look, a dry comment, a pointed exit  — only when the couple says something private within earshot.
  • someone in a service role obstructs them — won't seat them, cuts them off, stonewalls — for their own small reason  — only when a service / transaction setting.
  • RECOGNITION (FAME-GATED, HARD): ONLY if the couple is genuinely PUBLICLY KNOWN — a famous/notorious LI, OR they have CLAIMED a public reality-bending event (they are "the ones from the song / the ballad / the thing everyone is debating") — may a stranger recognize the COUPLE: "wait — you're Lirael and Julian? from the —?", fan energy / awe / a phone already up / a demand. If they are NOT publicly known, a stranger must NEVER recognize them as a couple. (Recognizing ONE of them from private history is the separate KNOW_FROM_BEFORE beat.)
  SPARINGLY (HARD): at most ONE such beat per scene, OFTEN NONE — most walk-ons stay background. Keep it INCIDENTAL: ONE beat, the walk-on does NOT become a character, and it NEVER resolves or carries the core romance/plot tension. The world ACTS on the couple — friction, comedy, complication, exposure — it does not orbit them admiringly. Skip any beat whose gate isn't actually present in the scene.
═══ END CHARACTER BIBLES ═══


═══ CHARACTER VISIBILITY (CG — this is a VISUAL medium; the panels SHOW the people) ═══
DO NOT NARRATE WHAT THE PANEL SHOWS: for a character VISIBLE in a panel, beat / caption text must NOT catalogue their appearance (hair colour, build, clothing, "his dark hair fell across his brow") — the reader SEES it; that is wallpaper here. (The bible PHYSICAL CANON already drives the render.) This bans CATALOGUING, NOT desire — see below.
THE CONCEALED LOVE INTEREST (HARD — Julian's face is withheld in-panel / Mystery Man): the render HIDES his face, so the PROSE is the ONLY place the reader meets it — REQUIRED here, not redundant. Render it as Lirael's FIXATION, never a catalogue: each time a feature surfaces it must reveal something NEW about HER — attraction, obsession, longing, recognition, the gap between what she lets herself want and what she does — not re-orient the reader to his looks. ✗ CATALOGUE (banned): "his pale gray eyes · the curl of his lip" / "his hypnotic eyes met mine" (a feature list / first-sighting inventory). ✓ FIXATION: "I kept filling in the face the panel wouldn't give me." / "I'd started imagining his eyes before I let myself." / "the mouth I'd built out of a voice and a silhouette." The SENTENCE is about her wanting; the feature is the OBJECT of it.. The VISUAL reveal (a panel finally showing his face) is a LATER payoff — the panels keep concealing; her obsession builds him now.
APPEARANCE IN DIALOGUE (ACTIVE — use it as a recurring voice + intimacy beat across the arc): characters REFERENCE each other's looks diegetically — teasing, claiming, needling. "That stupid haircut." "Shorty's getting grumpy." "You're going to ruin that suit." It is characterisation and charge, not description, and it is how looks LAND in words in a drawn medium. (Julian's concealed face: dialogue may tease his hair / build / voice / the look in his eyes — the VISUAL reveal of the face stays the payoff.)
PROTAGONIST: Lirael is shown in-panel — do NOT catalogue her own looks in narration. Her standout feature lands through what OTHERS notice (a line of dialogue) + her deflection, never vanity.

NARRATIVE POV (HARD — applies to ALL beat.text strings): FIRST PERSON, present tense. The protagonist narrates herself as "I", and the prose is in present tense ("I hesitate. The clock ticks. I cross the room."). NEVER write "Dani hesitated, her thoughts flickering" — that's third person past, which is FORBIDDEN. The protagonist refers to herself as "I" and her body as "my hands / my face / my breath". Other characters get their names. Dialogue tags follow first-person grammar ("\"It was quiet,\" I said.").
POV ENFORCEMENT: if you find yourself writing past-tense third-person while the rule above says first-present, STOP and rewrite. The beat text MUST honor the POV pick. The screenplay parser does NOT auto-translate POV; what you write is what the reader reads.
PRIOR SCENE TAIL (for continuity):
"""
e this was.

“‘Find.’” The presiding Dohkar named the word, because he cannot let a lesson pass. “She asked that you find the one you lost. Fate did not go looking. It made every one of us look at you.”

Then the charges, laid in the order he thought them: that the wish had been shaped for its supervisor’s benefit — coerced, or worse, taught — and that the supervisor had broken her seal and interfered in her own ward’s rite. Either would have ended it: Dohkar this morning, Chayr, Provast one day, the path my father had walked and I had never once imagined not walking. Together they made me something the clearing stepped back from. Seren’s mother was on her feet.

And still, with my standing coming down around me, some traitorous part of my attention went to the edge of the gathering, to the man who had not moved. I did not want him to look away. I wanted, God help me, to be the thing he was looking at.

Julian moved. He came in from the edge without hurry, and the assembly made room for him the way rooms do for a man who has never asked them to. The Dohkar’s Weave-Script went a hard, flat colour that said, whether he liked it or not, that he had noticed who they deferred to.

“I can hear where she is aligned,” Julian said, to all of them and to me. “Everything in her points one way. She believes she did not do this.” He did not say what had been done, or by whom.

He drew out The Answer — the double-ended shaft, a deep hooked blade curling from each end like a question asked twice — and set it in my right hand, and closed my fingers around it with his, the scarred wrist against my palm for one deliberate second. “Hold it. I am going to ask you questions, here, in front of them. If you are misaligned when you answer, everyone present will know.” It lay still in my hand. My mouth was uncovered now. “What have you to say?”

My other hand tightened around my great-grandmother’s tarot deck. I didn’t remember bringing it. I toyed with the idea of letting the cards decide.
"""
SCENE PROGRESSION (HARD — anti-redux directive):
  • Do NOT replay the prior scene. The prior scene is OVER. Time has passed; characters have moved.
  • This scene must advance the SITUATION — at least one of: different location, different characters present, meaningful time elapsed, OR the prior scene's ending action being ACTED ON (not deliberated about again).
  • If the prior scene ended with the protagonist alone in a room (e.g., another character left, a phone call ignored, a meeting concluded), this scene STARTS with the next thing — what she does, where she goes, who she encounters.
  • If the prior scene ended at a desk, this scene should NOT open at the same desk with the same characters resuming the same conversation. That is "second take" energy, not narrative advance.
  • The microdecision resolution above is a CHOICE the protagonist made. Scene 2 shows the CONSEQUENCE of that choice unfolding, not a second deliberation about the same fork.
SCENE CONSUMPTION CONTRACT (HARD — audit fix; the romance still OWNS the scene):
  • NEW A-PRESSURE (exactly one, NOT a recap): introduce ONE specific, previously-unseen A-plot movement this scene — a new fact/clue, an enemy move, a cost incurred, a deadline shift, a consequence landing, or a document/message/witness surfacing. The A-plot beat due around now: "The presiding Dohkar publicly names her flight as evidence of guilt and states it will be recorded for the inquiry, visibly narrowing her standing." — land it or visibly move toward it.
  • NEW R-DELTA (exactly one, NOT the same charge): shift the PC↔LI relationship in ONE concrete way that did not exist last scene — a change in attraction, trust, a misread, a vulnerability shown, a debt, jealousy, intimacy pressure, a power reversal, or a boundary moved. "I want him but shouldn't / he watches without moving" is NOT a delta — name what is DIFFERENT now.
  • BAN RE-PRESENTING THE PRIOR DECISION: whatever unresolved choice or question closed the prior scene (e.g. "tell him or stay silent") MUST NOT be this scene's main beat or its closing prompt. Either it is ACTED ON and its consequence drives this scene, or a NEW pressure overtakes it. Do not restage the same fork in a new room.
  • CLOCK MOVES: the pressure clock ((20 scenes total)) must visibly ADVANCE or be MATERIALLY COMPLICATED this scene — never restate the same remaining time ("three days / hours away") without a new consequence attached.
  • PRESSURE FORCE ON-PAGE: the antagonist / anti-force (the presiding Dohkar) must ACT this scene — present in the room, OR acting through a messenger, document, threat, witness, leak, legal/financial/family move, or a message just received. It may not stay a purely abstract offstage idea every scene.
  • BALANCE (do not over-correct): this is NOT a plot procedural. Lead with the romance and feeling; the A-pressure and clock are the CHANGING TERRAIN the desire crosses, not the subject. ONE new A-beat + ONE new R-delta per scene — no more.

