SCENE 3 MANDATED FRAME (HARD — overrides every other instruction about opening / closing beats):
- OPENING ARC (HARD — beats[0] through approximately beats[3-5]): the scene OPENS with the protagonist INVOKING PETITION FATE. The Petition mechanic emerged at the end of Scene 2 — she now uses it. Sequence within the opening beats:
   • She makes a small, IN-CHARACTER WISH using Petition Fate. The wish should be something natural to her current situation — a relational or work-context desire that fits the prior scenes' material (NOT cosmic / world-bending — those are Tempt territory). Examples: "I wish he'd call me back" / "I wish the meeting went my way" / "I wish I could see him alone again." Tailor the specific wish to the actual A-plot pressure / LI dynamic / prior-scene microdecision the protagonist made.
   • AN OMEN APPEARS: Fate signals it heard. Something small in the environment responds — a flicker, a sound, a coincidence at the edge of perception. NOT explicit (no voices, no glowing cards) — the omen is something the protagonist NOTICES but could plausibly dismiss.
   • THE WISH IS GRANTED IN A "WAS THAT COINCIDENCE?" WAY: the thing she wished for happens — but in a form that could plausibly have happened anyway. He calls, or the meeting tilts her way, or the door is unlocked — but in a way that leaves room for skepticism. The protagonist's reaction is CURIOSITY + SKEPTICISM, not certainty. She doesn't say "Fate is real." She thinks "Was that…? No. Coincidence."
   • DO NOT have the protagonist mentally narrate the system mechanic ("I used the Petition Fate card") — show the WISH and the OMEN and the GRANT through behavior, not exposition.
- MIDDLE: the scene develops with plot-acceleration pressure — whatever stakes the A-plot / LI dynamic generates. Standard scene structure; the second half should escalate toward the closer.
- The FINAL beat text MUST be EXACTLY this sentence, verbatim, no rewording, no substitution, kind = "narration", speaker = null:
    I realize the deck is still in my hand. As I slide Petition Fate back in, I notice a flash of red in the otherwise gold stack. “Tempt Fate.” Great-grandma never let me play with that one. A sword raised to a blood-red sky, lightning arcing down from the clouds (or maybe arcing up?) Ow! Static shock reminds me I need to pay attention to the present…
- STRUCTURAL MEANING OF the closer: the protagonist puts Petition back, and Tempt Fate REVEALS ITSELF in the deck. The static shock is Fate touching her — a small physical confirmation that the deck is NOT inert. The "pay attention to the present" half-line pulls her back into the room. The UI unlock animation for Tempt Fate fires after the player clicks Next. DO NOT continue the thought past the ellipsis — the card does that.
- The closer IS the last sentence of the scene.
- The deck stays in the frame across this scene (referenced incidentally) but the SPECIFIC "Tempt Fate" emergence prose appears ONLY in the closer.

DECK CANONICAL FACTS (HARD — observe whenever the deck appears anywhere in this scene's prose, not just the verbatim opener/closer):
  • SEVEN (7) cards total. Not seventy-eight, not twenty-two. Just seven. The deck is unlike any other tarot deck the protagonist has ever seen.
  • The cards are SOLID GOLD — heavy, weighty, metallic. Never paper. Never worn at the edges in the paper-deck sense. The wear they show is the wear gold takes — burnished, hand-warmed, faintly nicked.
  • The deck rests in a LEATHER SLEEVE. Never a silk bag. Never silk wrapping. Never cloth. Never a pouch. Never described as wrapped.
  • The deck belonged to the protagonist's GREAT-GRANDMOTHER, not her grandmother. Any family-lore reference attributes to the great-grandmother.
  • Great-grandmother's framing (surface ONLY if the scene naturally invites a family-lore line — never bolt it on as exposition): she used to say, joking but holding eye contact while she said it, that she didn't use the deck to READ the future — she used it to MAKE the future. The joke was always followed by a slow knowing nod, making sure you heard what she wasn't saying.
  • FORBIDDEN improvisations: silk bag, silk wrap, paper cards, worn-at-the-edges aging in the paper-deck sense, riffled shuffling, the smell of incense or sandalwood unless the protagonist's actual setting establishes them, generic fortune-telling tropes ("the cards never lie"), orientalist framing.


